# Security headers for every response. HSTS is already applied by Netlify
# at the edge for the custom domain, so it isn't repeated here.
#
# CSP notes:
# - script-src allows two fixed inline scripts by exact sha256 hash instead
#   of 'unsafe-inline': our own bootstrap script (adds the `js` class before
#   paint), and one Umami's script.js injects itself once it loads. That
#   second hash was read directly from a live CSP-violation report after
#   deploy, not guessed — if Umami ever changes what that inline script
#   contains, the hash will need to be refreshed the same way (deploy,
#   check the console for a new violation, copy its hash). Also allows the
#   GSAP/ScrollTrigger CDN and cloud.umami.is (serves script.js itself). The
#   inline JSON-LD block needs no allowance — script-src only governs
#   elements with a JavaScript MIME type, and application/ld+json isn't one.
# - style-src allows 'unsafe-inline' because the one <img style="..."> logo
#   sizing attribute and Google Fonts' returned stylesheet both need it;
#   there's no build step here to move either to a static class/nonce.
# - connect-src allows gateway.umami.is — confirmed via live testing that
#   this (not cloud.umami.is, which only serves the script file) is where
#   Umami actually sends pageview/event beacons. Otherwise nothing on this
#   page opens a fetch/XHR/websocket of its own. frame-src allows only
#   youtube-nocookie.com, for the click-to-play films on the festivals and
#   corporate pages (the iframe is only created after a visitor presses play;
#   thumbnails are self-hosted so img-src is unchanged). Because it names that
#   one origin instead of allowing frames generally. Note: Netlify's own
#   "Powered by Netlify" badge (`nl-badge-frame`) is injected as a srcdoc
#   iframe, which frame-src does not govern, so it still appears on the live
#   pages regardless of this directive (confirmed in the browser on 2026-10-07;
#   an earlier version of this comment wrongly claimed 'none' was hiding it).
/*
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  Permissions-Policy: camera=(), microphone=(), geolocation=(), payment=()
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' 'sha256-/x7W7R75k8Roq0WaVRQX9blP4OufE5xbAdzklGxsgpw=' 'sha256-mTJ4cJaTm2Gw95GeXEpZdvEEY9ybh6FZu1bwcNE7QlY=' https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com https://cloud.umami.is; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline' https://fonts.googleapis.com; font-src 'self' https://fonts.gstatic.com; img-src 'self' data:; media-src 'self'; connect-src 'self' https://gateway.umami.is; frame-src https://www.youtube-nocookie.com; object-src 'none'; base-uri 'self'; form-action 'self'

# Long-lived immutable caching for video assets — these are large background
# loops that change rarely; Netlify's default is
# `public,max-age=0,must-revalidate` (always-revalidate) which re-checks on
# every load for no benefit here. Filenames aren't content-hashed, so if a
# video is ever replaced, rename the file (or bump a `?v=` query the way
# CSS/JS already do) rather than overwriting the same path — otherwise
# returning visitors' browsers/CDN edges will keep serving the old bytes for
# up to a year.
/assets/video/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# CSS and JS are only ever referenced with a ?v=<date> suffix (in the HTML and
# in every JS import), so each version has its own URL and can be cached for a
# year. IMPORTANT: whenever a CSS/JS file changes, bump that suffix everywhere
# (grep the old value in *.html and js/*.js) — otherwise returning visitors
# keep the old file for up to a year.
/css/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable
/js/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable
